## 14. How to test whether the idea is actually good

Before adding content, ask five people who have never seen the game to try it.

Watch for these signals:

- They understand Pull and Push without a long explanation.
- They can create a satisfying collision within the first 10 seconds.
- They understand why they failed.
- They try again without being prompted.
- They discover at least one clever chain reaction on their own.

If players only hold a button and wait, the mechanic needs more agency. If the results feel random, physics needs more tuning. If they cannot predict what will happen, the visual feedback needs improvement.

The key metric is not how many features the game has. It's whether one simple action produces a satisfying result that makes the player want to try a more clever action next time.

