# MAGNET MAYHEM — Master Game Development Brief

## Objective

Build a complete, playable, polished 3D browser arcade game called MAGNET MAYHEM. The game must have a distinctive magnetic-force mechanic and must not resemble a traditional endless runner.

## Core gameplay

The player controls a small glowing magnetic orb in compact 3D arenas. There are two primary states:

- PULL: metal objects accelerate toward the orb.
- PUSH: a magnetic impulse sends nearby metal objects away from the orb and can propel the orb if the level rules allow it.

Use magnetic interactions to break barriers, hit targets, create chain reactions, activate mechanisms, and reach exits. Design gameplay around deliberate timing, predictable physics, and satisfying object interactions.

## Controls

Desktop: hold the left mouse button to Pull; use Space or the designated right-side input to activate/switch Push. Escape pauses the game.

Mobile: use large, clearly explained touch zones. Prevent browser scrolling, accidental zooming, and text selection during gameplay. Handle touch cancellation and focus loss safely.

Keep the control scheme consistent. Do not add complex combat controls.

## Initial scope

Deliver one complete mode with:

- One tutorial arena
- At least five carefully designed challenge arenas
- A small set of metal objects, targets, breakable barriers, and hazards
- Score, combos, objectives, success/failure conditions
- Main menu, pause menu, settings, results screen, restart
- Persistent best score and basic cosmetic unlocks
- Sound effects, music toggle, responsive UI
- Desktop and mobile-browser support

Prioritize one excellent mode over several incomplete modes. Structure the code so future modes can be added without rewriting the core game.

## Visual direction

Create a colorful, premium-looking stylized 3D toy factory/science laboratory. Use glossy materials, brushed metal, expressive animation, cyan Pull effects, orange Push effects, readable object silhouettes, particles, trails, and controlled camera reactions.

The orb and magnetic state must be visually unmistakable. Make the game attractive in screenshots and short video clips, but never sacrifice readability for visual effects.

## Physics and fairness

Use a browser-compatible physics engine if practical. Tune magnetic range, acceleration, impulse, mass classes, friction, damping, and collision response. Cap velocities and force values. Prevent objects from accumulating indefinitely or escaping the arena permanently.

Use object pooling for frequent particles and debris. Provide a predictable result for similar inputs. Clearly communicate danger and target states.

## Architecture

Use TypeScript, Vite, and Three.js unless the existing project has a compelling reason to retain another stack. Separate input, physics, game state, level configuration, UI, audio, progression, and rendering into maintainable modules.

Use explicit game states: MENU, TUTORIAL, PLAYING, PAUSED, VICTORY, GAME_OVER. Pausing must halt gameplay simulation. Restarting must fully reset all round-specific objects, timers, scoring, and physics.

Keep game simulation independent of rendering frame rate. Handle window resizing, visibility changes, asset loading, missing audio, and WebGL limitations gracefully.

## Persistence

Use local storage for settings, best scores, unlocked cosmetics, and progression. Validate stored data and provide safe defaults if it is missing or corrupted. Do not require sign-in or a server for the first release.

## Quality requirements

No fake buttons, placeholder gameplay, unimplemented menu items, or endless loading screens. Every visible control must work. The game must be playable from the first launch, with a concise interactive tutorial.

Test collision edge cases, repeated restarts, pause/resume, input release, mobile touch, object cleanup, and performance over extended play sessions.

## Development process

First inspect the existing project, if one exists. Preserve working code and identify the actual framework before making changes. Implement in milestones, run the game after each milestone, fix errors, and verify the playable result rather than merely describing it.

First deliver the smallest fun prototype. Then improve game feel, complete the core mode, polish the interface, and optimize performance. Do not claim a feature works until it has been implemented and tested.

## Definition of done

A new player can open the browser game, understand Pull and Push, create satisfying magnetic chain reactions, complete multiple challenges, see a meaningful score, restart instantly, and return later to find their saved progress. The game looks and feels like a coherent product, not a prototype made of disconnected features.

## My final recommendation

Build MAGNET MAYHEM, but validate the magnetic interaction before committing to the entire game. The mechanic has potential because it is simple to explain, naturally visual, and capable of producing unpredictable-looking but skill-driven moments.

The most important first milestone is a single arena where pulling a group of metal objects together and pushing them outward feels so satisfying that someone immediately wants to do it again. If that works, the rest of the game has a strong foundation.